1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:07,260
Hello everyone and welcome to another great lesson here at EnglishPod.

2
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:08,040
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:12,820
My name is Catherine and today we've got an upper intermediate level lesson for you all.

4
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:13,520
That's right.

5
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:21,780
We're going to talk a little bit about business and specifically we are in a business meeting where we're trying to sell something to someone.

6
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:22,260
That's right.

7
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:28,160
So you're going to hear a lot of talk about chocolate or in this case gourmet bonbons.

8
00:00:28,840 --> 00:00:29,280
Gourmet bonbons.

9
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:32,760
This is another word for chocolate candies or candies.

10
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:33,000
Okay.

11
00:00:33,760 --> 00:00:36,600
So a bonbon is kind of like round usually, right?

12
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:36,940
Usually, yes.

13
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:38,600
Okay.

14
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:39,600
All right.

15
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:40,720
So let's get started and listen to today's dialogue.

16
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:41,980
We'll be back in a moment.

17
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:52,360
I'm glad you could find time to meet with me, Mr. Johnson.

18
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:55,940
I can't think of a nicer environment for our meeting today.

19
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:57,280
The ambience here is lovely.

20
00:00:58,360 --> 00:00:59,440
No problem.

21
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,880
If possible, I always combine business with pleasure.

22
00:01:02,760 --> 00:01:04,780
Now let's hear more about these chocolates you're offering.

23
00:01:05,370 --> 00:01:12,490
Well, as you know, I have recently become the sole distributor for Granger's gourmet bonbons here in the United States.

24
00:01:13,110 --> 00:01:16,970
They're a new manufacturer and are looking to break into the luxury market.

25
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:20,150
Naturally, your restaurant sprang into my mind immediately.

26
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:32,110
I think your brand exemplifies many of the same traits as Granger's and serving these chocolates would really add to your reputation for providing elegant, luxurious, first-class dining.

27
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:33,430
Hmm.

28
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:34,530
Sounds interesting.

29
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:35,890
Gourmet chocolates.

30
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:37,270
Where are they produced?

31
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:38,760
Belgium?

32
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:41,000
Actually, the factory is located in Scotland.

33
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:42,120
Really?

34
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,600
I didn't think they were known for their luxury chocolate production.

35
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:49,320
That's what makes this such a fantastic opportunity.

36
00:01:49,980 --> 00:01:59,800
The government is 100% supportive of creating new export markets and has guaranteed a low tariff for all wholesale orders of over 1,000 units.

37
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:03,360
They've also reduced the red tape involved at customs as well.

38
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Here, I brought these especially for you.

39
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:06,700
Try one.

40
00:02:07,100 --> 00:02:07,960
Oh, thanks.

41
00:02:08,360 --> 00:02:09,360
Hmm.

42
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:10,360
Hmm.

43
00:02:11,280 --> 00:02:12,000
Creamy texture.

44
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:13,700
Very smooth.

45
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:15,100
Unique, aren't they?

46
00:02:15,420 --> 00:02:17,520
I bet you've never tasted anything like it.

47
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:23,920
Quality is assured as I personally visit the factory to make sure no one's cutting corners with the ingredients.

48
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,200
Only the creme de la creme make it through inspection.

49
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:27,100
Yes.

50
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:28,400
Very interesting flavors.

51
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:29,840
Slightly spicy.

52
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:30,840
Hmm.

53
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:32,560
Very unique, that's for sure.

54
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,640
Exactly what are the ingredients?

55
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:42,660
I have it on highest authority that this traditional secret recipe has been handed down in the Granger family for generations.

56
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:44,880
I'm sure you can keep a secret.

57
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:52,840
Buttermilk, cacao beans, sugar, and haggis.

58
00:02:52,840 --> 00:02:55,960
What's haggis?

59
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:57,790
It's a traditional Scottish delicacy.

60
00:02:58,360 --> 00:03:02,730
You take sheep's liver, heart, and lung and stuff it inside the sheep's stomach.

61
00:03:07,420 --> 00:03:09,060
I'll get back to you.

62
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:09,680
Mr. Johnson.

63
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:10,820
Mr. Johnson.

64
00:03:11,280 --> 00:03:12,280
All right.

65
00:03:19,910 --> 00:03:20,290
We're back.

66
00:03:20,570 --> 00:03:22,470
So a lot of interesting vocab there.

67
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:31,040
The guy was trying to convince them about these chocolates but it turned out to be something a little bit strange and we'll talk about that in a little while.

68
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:37,450
Before we get into that, why don't we take a look at seven key words that we have prepared for you today on Language Takeaway.

69
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Language Takeaway.

70
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:42,000
Okay.

71
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:45,440
So we've divided Language Takeaway into two parts today.

72
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:49,320
The first part is all about business and manufacturing.

73
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:50,340
So what's our first word, Marco?

74
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:51,040
All right.

75
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:57,600
So I talked about being the sole distributor for this company called Granger's Gourmet Bonbons.

76
00:03:57,600 --> 00:03:57,920
Okay.

77
00:03:58,600 --> 00:03:58,740
A distributor.

78
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:00,600
Okay.

79
00:04:00,600 --> 00:04:08,880
This is a person or a company and the distributor is someone who distributes or sells a product, right?

80
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:09,880
Right.

81
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:10,880
So the factory will make them.

82
00:04:10,880 --> 00:04:18,160
He will get all these goods from the factory and then he will be the one that gives it to the different retail stores.

83
00:04:18,720 --> 00:04:22,940
So he can have many clients and he is the main person who you get it from.

84
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:25,000
So obviously they don't get it directly from the factory.

85
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:25,640
Okay.

86
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:30,480
So this person, a distributor or a company, is in the middle of the transaction.

87
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:35,920
On one side there is the manufacturer and on the other side there is the retail market or stores.

88
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:36,720
Exactly.

89
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:40,700
And as you said, that's our second key word today, manufacturer.

90
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:42,000
Okay.

91
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:45,860
Manufacturer, again, is a person or a company that makes a good.

92
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:49,720
So for example, a television manufacturer makes TV.

93
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:50,660
It's like a factory.

94
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:51,300
Exactly.

95
00:04:51,940 --> 00:04:53,520
So they manufacture things.

96
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:54,320
They make them.

97
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:55,680
All right.

98
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:57,800
So we've got distributors who are moving goods.

99
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,760
We have manufacturers who are making them.

100
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:03,380
But what happens when we want to move a good out of a country?

101
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:04,160
Okay.

102
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,740
So that is our third word for today.

103
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:12,580
Usually in customs, we have to pay a tariff for these goods or a tax.

104
00:05:13,140 --> 00:05:13,400
Okay.

105
00:05:13,460 --> 00:05:22,220
So a tariff is a special kind of tax that you pay when you are trying to export or send outside of your country goods that you've made in that country.

106
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:30,600
So for example, if I make 100 TVs in America, but I want to sell these TVs to someone in Mexico, I have to pay a tariff.

107
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:38,240
I have to pay like $10 to the government that allows me to send these out of the country.

108
00:05:38,460 --> 00:05:38,980
That's right.

109
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:40,540
So that is a tariff.

110
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:53,120
And now when he was talking about tariffs and how the government is reducing the tariffs in order to motivate a business, he talked about also that the government is reducing or cutting the red tape.

111
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:53,840
Okay.

112
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:58,220
So he says, listen, they've also reduced the red tape involved at customs.

113
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:09,120
So this means that before they were encountering some problems at customs, because customs, you know, there's a lot of paperwork, there's a lot of taxes, there's a lot of information you have to provide.

114
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:10,440
It's a hassle.

115
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:11,880
A lot of paperwork.

116
00:06:12,280 --> 00:06:12,400
Right.

117
00:06:12,620 --> 00:06:16,940
So this kind of paperwork, bureaucracy, this is called red tape in English.

118
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:27,660
For example, I would love to get a visa for my foreign husband, but there's a lot of red tape.

119
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,540
There's a lot of work that we have to do.

120
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:32,120
There's a lot of bureaucratic processes that you have to do.

121
00:06:32,380 --> 00:06:32,680
All right.

122
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:38,080
So red tape, this means a lot of paperwork, a lot of work that you have to do.

123
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:39,080
Okay.

124
00:06:39,080 --> 00:06:41,240
So those are our key words that are related to business.

125
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,720
Now let's move on to a couple of different adjectives.

126
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:48,360
The first one, when he talked about chocolates, he said elegant.

127
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:50,200
He said that something is elegant.

128
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:51,340
Okay.

129
00:06:51,660 --> 00:06:51,820
Elegant.

130
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:53,080
This is a great adjective.

131
00:06:53,500 --> 00:06:57,660
You're going to see this on a lot of luxury brand items.

132
00:06:58,020 --> 00:07:03,120
Elegance means something is elegant if it is classy, if it looks really good and rich.

133
00:07:03,140 --> 00:07:09,300
Or you could say that dress is very elegant, that mink coat is very elegant.

134
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:09,760
Okay.

135
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:11,400
This is a positive thing.

136
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:14,420
So something that looks kind of expensive at times as well.

137
00:07:14,620 --> 00:07:15,200
Yeah, that's right.

138
00:07:15,790 --> 00:07:20,290
Another word that means looking expensive or elegant is luxurious.

139
00:07:21,130 --> 00:07:21,410
Okay.

140
00:07:21,450 --> 00:07:25,810
So luxurious comes from the noun luxury.

141
00:07:26,230 --> 00:07:26,690
That's right.

142
00:07:26,690 --> 00:07:27,870
Luxury, luxurious.

143
00:07:28,910 --> 00:07:33,290
So we can say that the executive suites at this hotel are very luxurious.

144
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:38,370
They have a jacuzzi, a king-size bed, expensive silk sheets.

145
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:41,040
The best quality materials.

146
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:45,120
So you can say that elegant and luxurious kind of go hand in hand.

147
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:45,980
That's right.

148
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:56,420
Although elegant, you usually say about someone or their clothing or even a car, but luxurious is more like a place or an object.

149
00:07:57,120 --> 00:07:57,600
Okay, good.

150
00:07:58,240 --> 00:08:05,600
And then we have one final word that also describes something that is very rare or very good and rich, a delicacy.

151
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:08,000
Yeah, actually this one is a noun, it's a thing.

152
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:13,760
So we can say that this is a Scottish delicacy or what are the delicacies in your country?

153
00:08:14,360 --> 00:08:16,880
So delicacy is something that's special.

154
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:17,140
Okay.

155
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:23,190
So for example, you've told me that in Ecuador, there are some interesting food delicacies.

156
00:08:23,240 --> 00:08:24,240
Delicacies?

157
00:08:24,240 --> 00:08:25,240
Little animals.

158
00:08:25,240 --> 00:08:25,410
Yeah.

159
00:08:26,240 --> 00:08:26,550
For example, guinea pigs.

160
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:33,960
So it's not really a delicacy, although kind of because guinea pigs are expensive comparatively to chickens, for example.

161
00:08:33,960 --> 00:08:38,360
So it is considered to be something that you only eat on special occasions.

162
00:08:38,390 --> 00:08:38,570
Right.

163
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:44,120
And in East Asia, many people consider shark's fin soup to be a delicacy.

164
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:45,120
Right.

165
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:46,640
It's a special dish.

166
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:54,260
So we can say that it's usually about food and it's rare, something that's rare or special or also can be very expensive.

167
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:55,920
That's right, a delicacy.

168
00:08:56,680 --> 00:08:57,680
Okay.

169
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:59,920
So a lot of great interesting words on language takeaway.

170
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:04,680
Why don't we move on now to fluency builder?

171
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:04,990
Fluency builder.

172
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:10,900
This first phrase is actually something I try to do as often as possible, but it's usually very difficult.

173
00:09:11,380 --> 00:09:13,260
I combine business with pleasure.

174
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:13,800
Right.

175
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:15,660
To combine business with pleasure.

176
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:25,120
Now this is a whole phrase and this talks about doing business, but at the same time, doing it in a fun way or an interesting way.

177
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:25,620
Right.

178
00:09:26,440 --> 00:09:30,560
And pleasure here means something that is fun or entertainment even.

179
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:36,290
So for example, going out to dinner with your company is a way to combine business with pleasure.

180
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:38,120
All right.

181
00:09:38,120 --> 00:09:44,040
Or meeting a client at a bar is combining business with pleasure.

182
00:09:44,040 --> 00:09:47,250
So you're combining things that are fun and things that are work related.

183
00:09:47,280 --> 00:09:48,280
Right.

184
00:09:48,280 --> 00:10:00,410
Because you can have a business meeting in a boardroom or in a business or in a meeting room, but if you have the same business meeting at a bar where you're talking and laughing, but also at the same time talking business, then you're combining business with pleasure.

185
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:01,150
That's right.

186
00:10:01,960 --> 00:10:05,030
And now for our second phrase, it's more about cheating, right?

187
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:08,970
Somebody that is cheating or maybe not doing things correctly.

188
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:10,950
They are cutting corners.

189
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:11,690
That's right.

190
00:10:12,560 --> 00:10:14,590
So cutting corners is another way to say cheating.

191
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:21,170
For example, I've seen people, students cutting corners with their studies.

192
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:25,330
You think about cutting corners, it means that you're not going the long way.

193
00:10:25,560 --> 00:10:28,520
You're cutting, you're cheating by going the shorter way.

194
00:10:28,520 --> 00:10:29,050
You're taking a shortcut.

195
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:30,520
Taking a shortcut.

196
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:37,690
So for example, instead of studying for that final test in math, some people write the formulas on their hands.

197
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:39,550
This is a way to cut corners.

198
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:43,750
Instead of studying, you actually cheat by putting it on your hand, making it easier.

199
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:49,800
So to cut corners is to cheat or to try to take the shortcut, but it kind of has a negative connotation, right?

200
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:50,240
Oh, definitely.

201
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:53,180
So most people will say, don't cut corners.

202
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:54,760
In the end, it's not worth it.

203
00:10:55,400 --> 00:10:56,400
Right.

204
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:58,800
And for our final phrase, we have one that it's in French.

205
00:10:59,740 --> 00:11:03,320
So this one is in French, but it's also a very common English phrase.

206
00:11:03,460 --> 00:11:04,900
That's why we're going to teach it to you.

207
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:07,080
The creme de la creme.

208
00:11:07,080 --> 00:11:08,080
Okay.

209
00:11:08,080 --> 00:11:12,380
So literally, in translated from French, that means the cream of the cream.

210
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:13,720
Right.

211
00:11:13,720 --> 00:11:16,120
In English, we would say that it's the best of the best.

212
00:11:16,320 --> 00:11:16,850
The best of the best.

213
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:17,730
The very top.

214
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:19,850
So that's what this phrase means.

215
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:21,230
The best of the best.

216
00:11:21,430 --> 00:11:22,010
The very top.

217
00:11:22,010 --> 00:11:23,890
The most excellent.

218
00:11:24,490 --> 00:11:30,610
So you could say, Harvard University attracts the creme de la creme of American high school students.

219
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:32,640
Okay.

220
00:11:32,640 --> 00:11:34,000
So the very best of American high school students.

221
00:11:34,170 --> 00:11:34,570
All right.

222
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,880
So that phrase, it's in French, but you can say it in English, right?

223
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:40,560
Like fiance, for example.

224
00:11:40,560 --> 00:11:41,560
Fiance.

225
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:41,990
Creme de la creme.

226
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:45,640
So those are the words that we've stolen from French and we use in English.

227
00:11:45,640 --> 00:11:47,550
So don't worry about how you pronounce it in English.

228
00:11:47,590 --> 00:11:49,230
We just say creme de la creme.

229
00:11:49,290 --> 00:11:49,710
Okay.

230
00:11:49,790 --> 00:11:54,550
So why don't we listen to this dialogue for the last time, and we'll be back in just a bit.

231
00:12:01,560 --> 00:12:04,760
I'm glad you could find time to meet with me, Mr. Johnson.

232
00:12:04,820 --> 00:12:07,560
I can't think of a nicer environment for our meeting today.

233
00:12:07,820 --> 00:12:08,900
The ambience here is lovely.

234
00:12:09,580 --> 00:12:10,240
No problem.

235
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:13,460
If possible, I always combine business with pleasure.

236
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:16,340
Now let's hear more about these chocolates you're offering.

237
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:24,040
Well, as you know, I have recently become the sole distributor for Granger's Gourmet Bonbons here in the United States.

238
00:12:24,580 --> 00:12:28,540
They're a new manufacturer and are looking to break into the luxury market.

239
00:12:29,160 --> 00:12:31,720
Naturally, your restaurant sprang into my mind immediately.

240
00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:43,680
I think your brand exemplifies many of the same traits as Granger's, and serving these chocolates would really add to your reputation for providing elegant, luxurious, first-class dining.

241
00:12:44,620 --> 00:12:46,140
Hmm, sounds interesting.

242
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:47,400
Gourmet chocolates.

243
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:48,850
Where are they produced?

244
00:12:49,320 --> 00:12:50,320
Belgium?

245
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:52,590
Actually, the factory is located in Scotland.

246
00:12:53,250 --> 00:12:53,690
Really?

247
00:12:54,250 --> 00:12:57,190
I didn't think they were known for their luxury chocolate production.

248
00:12:57,650 --> 00:13:00,870
That's what makes this such a fantastic opportunity.

249
00:13:01,540 --> 00:13:11,390
